A max-heap gives the largest value first. After removing all copies of that value, the next heap value is the second largest distinct number. A `priority_queue` stores the array values, so duplicate maximum values are skipped in a loop.

This code does not modify `nums`, but it uses extra memory for the heap.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int secondLargestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        priority_queue<int> values;
        for (int value : nums) {
            values.push(value);
        }

        int largest = values.top();
        while (!values.empty() && values.top() == largest) {
            values.pop();
        }

        if (values.empty()) {
            return -1;
        }
        return values.top();
    }
};
```

For `[9, 2, 9, 5]`, the heap returns `9` first. Both copies of `9` are removed, leaving `5` at the top.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for heap insertion and removal.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the priority queue.

This version first finds the largest value, then makes another pass to find the greatest value that is smaller than it. It is a little less compact than one-pass tracking, but the two steps are easy to follow.

The `foundSecond` flag is useful when the numbers can be negative, because `-1` might be a valid array value even though it is also the required failure result.

```go
func secondLargestElement(nums []int) int {
    if len(nums) < 2 {
        return -1
    }

    largest := nums[0]
    for _, value := range nums[1:] {
        if value > largest {
            largest = value
        }
    }

    foundSecond := false
    second := 0
    for _, value := range nums {
        if value < largest && (!foundSecond || value > second) {
            second = value
            foundSecond = true
        }
    }

    if !foundSecond {
        return -1
    }
    return second
}
```

Dry run for `[10, 6, 10, 3]`: the first scan finds `10`. The second scan ignores both `10` values and keeps `6` as the largest smaller value.

If all values are equal, the second scan finds nothing and returns `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)` from the two linear scans.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Sorting a copy keeps the logic short. After sorting from largest to smallest, compare each value with the largest value and return the first different one. This also handles duplicate maximum values correctly.

The original vector is not changed because the code sorts a separate copy.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int secondLargestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        vector<int> sortedNums = nums;
        sort(sortedNums.begin(), sortedNums.end(), greater<int>());

        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(sortedNums.size()); i++) {
            if (sortedNums[i] != sortedNums[0]) {
                return sortedNums[i];
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

For `[7, 7, 4, 2]`, the sorted copy is `[7, 7, 4, 2]`. The first value different from `7` is `4`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied vector.

A `TreeSet` removes duplicates and keeps the remaining values in sorted order. That makes the second largest value easy to get from the set iterator.

If the set has fewer than two values, all array elements were equal or the input was too short, so the result is `-1`.

```java
import java.util.TreeSet;

class Solution {
    public int secondLargestElement(int[] nums) {
        TreeSet<Integer> values = new TreeSet<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            values.add(value);
        }

        if (values.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        return values.lower(values.last());
    }
}
```

Dry run with `[5, 1, 5, 3]`: the set becomes `{1, 3, 5}`. The largest value is `5`, and the value just below it is `3`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for inserting values into the tree.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case for distinct values.

