## Intuition
The key insight is that we don't need to sort the array to find the second-largest element.

While traversing the array, we can keep track of two values:

large — the largest element found so far
scndlarge — the second-largest distinct element found so far

Whenever we find a new largest element, the previous large automatically becomes the second largest.
## Approach
Initialize large with the first element of the array.
Initialize scndlarge with Integer.MIN_VALUE.
Traverse the array from the second element.
If the current element is greater than large:
Move the current large into scndlarge.
Update large with the current element.
Otherwise, if the current element is greater than scndlarge and different from large, update scndlarge.
After traversing the entire array:
If scndlarge is still Integer.MIN_VALUE, there is no distinct second-largest element, so return -1.
Otherwise, return scndlarge.

The important condition nums[i] != large ensures that duplicate occurrences of the largest value are not considered the second-largest element.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int secondLargestElement(int[] nums) {
        int large=nums[0];
        int scndlarge=Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for(int i=1;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(large<nums[i]){
                scndlarge=large;
                large=nums[i];
            }
            else if(scndlarge<nums[i] && nums[i]!=large){
                scndlarge=nums[i];
            }
        }
        if(scndlarge == Integer.MIN_VALUE) return -1;
        return scndlarge;
    }
}
```

